;;; silent-non-errors.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · error-cards
;;; Census key: (none)   ·   family: silent-non-error   ·   silent-non-error-uncounted
;;; The most dangerous class: NOTHING is thrown. Nothing is counted. A wrong
;;; value is returned as if it were right.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "error-card — the silent non-errors (no throw, no census)")

(error-card :key "silent-non-error"
  :name "Silent non-errors (wrong value, no throw)"
  :family "silent-non-error"
  :census-status "silent-non-error-uncounted"
  :library "error-cards"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :provenance "scheme/reference/error-cards/silent-non-errors.slatl"
  :status "real"

  :scheme-classification
"None. That is the point. A strict Scheme would signal a type error for most of
these; Motoi, running on a JavaScript host, instead COERCES or DROPS and returns
a value. No condition is raised, so `guard` never fires and the error census
never counts. These are not in the census because they are not errors — they are
wrong ANSWERS."

  :trigger-code "(display (string-length 5))"
  :observed-output "1"
  :normalizes-to "(uncounted — nothing is thrown)"

  :meaning
"`(string-length 5)` should be a type error: 5 is not a string. Instead the host
coerces `5` to the string `\"5\"`, takes its length, and returns `1`. The program
runs to completion and prints a plausible-looking number. Nothing warns you. This
is the failure mode that does NOT show up in `stats/error-*` at all, because
Motoi never raised anything.

The census is a WRONG SINK for THROWN wrongs. Silent non-errors drain into no
sink — they are the blind spot the census cannot see. That is why this card
exists: to name the blind spot explicitly so a reader (human or model) learns to
suspect a RESULT even when there was no error."

  ;; A catalogue of observed silent non-errors — each verified, each returns a
  ;; wrong-ish value with no throw. This is the honest floor of Motoi today.
  :observed-catalogue (
    (:code "(display (string-length 5))"        :output "1"        :why "5 coerced to \"5\"; length 1")
    (:code "(display (+ 1 \"x\"))"               :output "1x"       :why "host string concat, not a numeric error")
    (:code "(display (cons 1 2 3))"              :output "(1 2)"    :why "extra argument silently dropped")
    (:code "(display (/ 1 0))"                   :output "+inf.0"   :why "division by zero yields +inf.0, not an error")
    (:code "(display (sqrt -4))"                 :output "+nan.0"   :why "no complex tower; negative root is NaN")
    (:code "(display (modulo 5 0))"              :output "+nan.0"   :why "modulo by zero yields NaN, not an error")
    (:code "(display ((lambda (x y) x) 1))"      :output "1"        :why "missing arg not checked; y is absent, x returned")
    (:code "(display ((lambda (x) x) 1 2 3))"    :output "1"        :why "extra args dropped; no arity error")
    (:code "(display (vector-ref (vector 1) 8))" :output ""         :why "out-of-range read returns nothing, no error")
    (:code "(display (car 42))"                  :output ""         :why "car of a non-pair returns nothing, no error"))

  :common-causes (
    "handing a number where a string is expected (or vice-versa) and getting a
     coerced answer instead of a type error"
    "arithmetic that a strict Scheme would reject — divide/modulo by zero, root of
     a negative — yielding +inf.0 / +nan.0"
    "calling a lambda with the wrong number of arguments — extra dropped, missing
     left undefined, no arity check"
    "reading past the end of a vector, or `car`/`cdr` of a non-pair, and getting an
     empty/undefined result rather than an error")

  :how-to-detect
"Since nothing is thrown, you must CHECK, not catch. Validate types and ranges at
the boundary yourself, and test for the tell-tale wrong values (`+nan.0`,
`+inf.0`, empty output):"
  :how-to-detect-code
"(define (checked-string-length x)
  (if (string? x) (string-length x) (error \"not a string\" x)))
(display (guard (e (#t (error-object-message e)))
  (checked-string-length 5)))"
  :how-to-detect-output "not a string"

  :fix-shape
"Add the guard the language does not: test `(string? x)`, `(pair? x)`,
`(> (vector-length v) i)`, `(not (zero? d))` BEFORE the operation, and raise your
own `(error ...)` when the precondition fails. That converts a silent wrong answer
into a thrown, catchable, COUNTED error — moving the failure out of the blind spot
and into the census where the wrong sink can see it."

  :lesson
"The scariest failures make no noise. Motoi's host-level coercions mean several
classic type errors return a plausible value instead of throwing, and those never
touch the error census. Two disciplines follow. First, distrust results that
COULD have come from a coercion — a `1` from `string-length`, an `+inf.0`, a
`+nan.0`. Second, when correctness matters, add your own precondition `error`s so
the silent wrong becomes a loud, countable one. You cannot mine a sink for wrongs
that were never thrown into it; the fix is to start throwing them."

  :census-verbs (
    "(stats/error-summary)   ;; these will NOT appear here — that is the point"
    "predicates you write yourself: string? pair? vector? zero? number?")

  :related-broken-examples ("hash-table-ref-value-default.slatl" "reduce-arg-order.slatl")
  :related-cards ("not-a-procedure" "roster-not-observed"))
